# theritesite/wp-plugin-build
#
# This directory is TWO things at once, and the .gitignore is what keeps them
# apart:
#
#   1. A parking lot for ~35 plugin working directories. Sixteen of those are
#      independent git repositories with their own remotes (theritesite/cog-wc,
#      theritesite/wc-net-profit, and so on). None of them belong to this repo.
#   2. The shared build tooling that all of them import. That IS this repo.
#
# DENY BY DEFAULT, then allow the few shared files back in.
#
# Why this way round rather than listing the 35 directories: every directory
# here is somebody else's repo, so the correct default for anything new is "not
# ours." A plugin cloned into this folder next month is ignored automatically
# instead of silently becoming a tracked subtree of this one. Listing the
# exclusions instead would mean this file needs an edit every time a plugin
# arrives, and the failure mode of forgetting is much worse - an accidental
# 1 GB commit of somebody else's node_modules.
#
# THE COST, so it does not surprise you later: adding a new shared file means
# adding an explicit "!" line below. If a file you just created never shows up
# in `git status`, this is why. `git check-ignore -v <file>` will confirm it.

/*

!/.gitignore
!/README.md
!/.github/
!/trs-build-targets.js
!/trs-package.js
!/trs-verify-versions.js
!/trs-deliver.js
!/trs-verify-build.js
!/.trs-build.example.json
!/template/

# NOT allowed back in, on purpose: .trs-build.json holds absolute paths for one
# specific machine. It is per-machine config, not shared code. Copy the example
# and edit it - see README.md.

# THE ONE SUBTREE, and what it costs.
#
# `/*` denies top-level entries only, so re-allowing /template/ re-allows
# everything inside it too. That is the intent - the template is a directory of
# shared files, not a single file - but it does mean this directory no longer
# has the "anything new here is somebody else's, so ignore it" property that the
# other 34 have. Nothing gets cloned into template/, so the realistic risk is
# not a stray repo; it is build junk from someone copying the template in place
# and running npm there. The rules below cover exactly that, and nothing else.

/template/**/node_modules/
/template/**/dist/
/template/**/zip_files/
/template/**/.DS_Store
/template/**/package-lock.json
/template/**/.trs-build.json
